<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="041" project="ai-advanced-capabilities-analysis-hub-r1">
  <metadata>
    <title>Wire activeWorkType host prop → getToolsForSurface scoping</title>
    <phase>4 Wizard</phase>
    <gate>deps 040 complete</gate>
    <status>not-started</status>
    <rigor>STANDARD</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>Modify-only wiring of an existing host prop to the already-shipped getToolsForSurface; no new surface, small blast radius; STANDARD per §8. ui-tests still required (frontend).</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>sonnet</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Pass-through prop wiring against a shipped function (ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490); fully specified, low ambiguity — sonnet default.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <parallel-group>P4</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Small distinct prop-wiring surface following 040; no .claude/ writes; parallel-safe within W4 after the wizard exists.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>040</deps>
    <tags>frontend, spaarkeai</tags>
    <estimated-effort>1-2 hours</estimated-effort>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>
    Wire the activeWorkType host prop so that launching Agreement Review scopes the tool palette to
    agreement-analysis tools. The host passes activeWorkType='agreement-analysis' when launching; ComposeEditor's prop
    defaults to '*'; the palette narrows via the already-shipped getToolsForSurface(surface, activeWorkType) at
    ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490. This is pass-through wiring — the scoping function already exists.
  </prompt>

  <role>
    SPAARKE platform developer. Familiar with ComposeEditor / ComposeAiToolbar props and the getToolsForSurface tool
    scoping already shipped in Spaarke.Compose.Components. Follow ADRs strictly.
  </role>

  <goal>
    The host passes activeWorkType='agreement-analysis' on launch; ComposeEditor threads it through (default '*') to
    ComposeAiToolbar, which calls the shipped getToolsForSurface(surface, activeWorkType) so the palette shows only
    agreement-analysis (+ '*') tools; ui-tests confirm the scoping in light and dark mode.
  </goal>

  <context>
    <background>
      FR-13: getToolsForSurface(surface, activeWorkType) already exists (ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490) and filters tools by
      `surfaces ∋ surface AND (workTypes ∋ '*' OR workTypes ∋ activeWorkType)`. ComposeEditor exposes an activeWorkType
      prop (default '*'). This task only threads the host launch value ('agreement-analysis') into that prop so the
      palette scopes correctly — no new scoping logic is written.
    </background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="canonical-reference">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeAiToolbar.tsx</file>
      <file role="modify">the host launch path that mounts ComposeEditor for Agreement Review (Spaarke.AI.Widgets launch/wizard wiring from task 040)</file>
      <file role="modify">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeEditor.tsx (thread activeWorkType if not already passed)</file>
    </relevant-files>
    <dependencies>
      <dependency task="040" status="pending">Per-type creation wizard — the Agreement Review launch path that will pass activeWorkType.</dependency>
    </dependencies>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="project">SCOPE: reuse the shipped scoping. Call the existing getToolsForSurface(surface, activeWorkType) at ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490; do NOT reimplement tool filtering.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">SCOPE: the prop default. ComposeEditor's activeWorkType prop defaults to '*' (all tools); only the Agreement Review launch overrides it to 'agreement-analysis'. Do NOT change the default or break unscoped surfaces.</constraint>
    <constraint source="ADR-021">SCOPE: the palette UI. The scoped palette renders correctly in light and dark Fluent themes (verify via ui-tests dark-mode check).</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <files>
      <file>.claude/adr/ADR-021-fluent-v9.md</file>
      <file>docs/standards/ASSISTANT-UI-ELEMENT-CRITERIA.md</file>
    </files>
    <patterns>
      <pattern name="getToolsForSurface" location="src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeAiToolbar.tsx">
        Shipped scoping: filters by surface AND (workTypes ∋ '*' OR workTypes ∋ activeWorkType). Pass the host's activeWorkType through — do not modify the function.
      </pattern>
    </patterns>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0" name="Context + rigor declaration">Declare RIGOR LEVEL: STANDARD (modify-only wiring). Confirm ComposeEditor's activeWorkType prop and the getToolsForSurface signature at ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490.</step>
    <step order="1">In the Agreement Review launch path (from task 040), pass activeWorkType='agreement-analysis' when mounting ComposeEditor.</step>
    <step order="2">Ensure ComposeEditor threads activeWorkType (default '*') into ComposeAiToolbar's getToolsForSurface call.</step>
    <step order="3">Add ui-tests (Agreement Review launch scopes palette to agreement-analysis tools; unscoped default still shows all; ADR-021 dark-mode) and run them.</step>
    <step order="4">Update TASK-INDEX.md: set 041 status to ✅.</step>
    <step order="5">Document any deviation in projects/ai-advanced-capabilities-analysis-hub-r1/notes/.</step>
  </steps>

  <ui-tests>
    <test name="Agreement Review scopes palette">
      <steps><step>Launch Agreement Review (host passes activeWorkType='agreement-analysis').</step></steps>
      <expected>The tool palette shows only agreement-analysis (+ '*') tools; tools scoped to other work types are hidden.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="Default is unscoped">
      <steps><step>Mount ComposeEditor without an activeWorkType override.</step></steps>
      <expected>activeWorkType defaults to '*' and the palette shows the full unscoped tool set — no regression on non-Agreement surfaces.</expected>
    </test>
    <test name="ADR-021 dark-mode">
      <steps><step>Render the scoped palette under the dark Fluent theme.</step></steps>
      <expected>The palette renders with correct contrast using Fluent v9 tokens.</expected>
    </test>
  </ui-tests>

  <tools>
    <tool name="npm">Build/test the shared lib + widget (build:prod for production output).</tool>
  </tools>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">the Agreement Review launch path (activeWorkType='agreement-analysis')</output>
    <output type="code">src/client/shared/Spaarke.Compose.Components/src/widgets/ComposeEditor.tsx (activeWorkType threading if needed)</output>
    <output type="test">tool-scoping ui-test alongside ComposeAiToolbar / the launch path</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">Given Agreement Review is launched, when the palette renders, then it is scoped to agreement-analysis (+ '*') tools via getToolsForSurface (FR-13 acceptance).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Given no activeWorkType override, when ComposeEditor mounts, then the prop defaults to '*' and the full tool set shows (no regression).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">getToolsForSurface at ComposeAiToolbar.tsx:490 is reused unchanged; no tool-filtering logic is reimplemented.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: an unrecognized activeWorkType value scopes to '*'-only tools rather than throwing.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">ADR-021: the scoped palette renders correctly in both light and dark themes.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">All unit/ui tests pass; shared-lib build is green.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Spec FR-13 (§137-139). Pure wiring — the scoping function is already shipped; this task only passes the host launch
    value into the existing prop. Modify-only, so no §11 justification required.
  </notes>

  <execution>
    <skill>.claude/skills/task-execute/SKILL.md</skill>
    <protocol>Load knowledge files. Follow task-execute at STANDARD rigor (Step 9.5 gates skipped per §8).</protocol>
  </execution>
</task>
